[0m[38;5;231m● [0mInspection complete, captain — first 20 lines read, nothing modified.

  1. [0m[1mA growing reply is one message, not many. [0mThe moment the assistant begins
     answering, the gateway should create exactly one conversation row bound to that
     turn and then keep rewriting that same row as more text arrives, so its identity
     never depends on the words it currently contains. Every snapshot of the
     terminal is a fresh observation of one evolving message, which means the correct
     operation is always an update in place rather than an append — and the row is
     only considered final when the turn itself is observed to close, never when a
     single snapshot happens to look complete.
  2. [0m[1mInterleaved machinery must be classified, not concatenated.[0m While a reply is
     still growing, tool invocations, status lines, and harness chrome appear in the
     same terminal stream and frequently land in the middle of the prose, so the 
     classifier has to type each row on its own merits before anything is written to
     the conversation record. A row it cannot confidently identify as assistant prose
     must be excluded or typed as tool activity rather than folded into the visible
     message, because an unrecognized fragment silently glued to the answer is worse
     than one omitted from it.
  3. [0m[1mDelivery must push every revision, and the client must trust the newest one.[0m
